\section{Introduction}
Yau (ISO 639-3 yuw; Glottocode yaum1237) is a Finisterre-Huon (Nuclear Trans New Guinea) language of Morobe Province, Papua New Guinea. It must not be confused with the unrelated Torricelli language Yau (ISO yyu) of Sandaun Province. Sources disagree on how Yau relates to Nungon and Uruwa: some treat Yau as the dialect group of the northeastern villages, with Nungon as the southeastern dialects. Little material specific to Yau was found.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No Yau primers, textbooks or school materials were found in search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia entry on this Yau language: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yau_language_(Trans%E2%80%93New_Guinea)}
\item Grambank record for yaum1237, citing Sarvasy's work on Nungon: \url{https://grambank.clld.org/languages/yaum1237}
\item Western Sydney University record, \emph{Nungon narratives} by Roslyn \"Ogate and Fooyu Finisterre Huon Morob. Nungon is the closely related southeastern variety: \url{https://researchers.westernsydney.edu.au/en/publications/nungon-narratives-by-roslyn-%C3%B6gate-and-fooyu-finisterre-huon-morob/}
\item Numerals page for Yau, Morobe: \url{https://lingweb.eva.mpg.de/channumerals/Yau-Morobe.htm}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
None found specific to Yau. No Yau dictionary appeared in search results.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
The language is reported as stable and spoken by about 1,700 people, but no school use was found. Any curriculum would probably start from Nungon documentation, notably Hannah Sarvasy's grammar. That route needs checking with the community, since the dialect relationship is unclear.
